From fea05e644162eeea7f8a6e3ce81b489df2faa123 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 12:08:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] privacy: add an NFC page

There is no NFC control anywhere in GNOME. The hardware is common on phones and
present on plenty of laptops, neard drives it on every distribution that ships
it, and a user's only way to turn the radio on or off is busctl.

This is the camera page with the parts that do not apply removed. Two
differences, both forced by NFC being real hardware where the camera flag is
not:

  - The switch is bound to org.neard.Adapter Powered over the system bus, not
    to a GSetting. disable-camera exists because a camera has no power switch;
    an NFC adapter has one, and binding to it means the switch reflects the
    radio even when something else changes it.

  - The page hides itself when neard reports no adapter, and the row in the
    panel follows it. Nearly every machine has a camera, so that page is always
    shown; nearly no machine has NFC.

The per-app permission list the camera page carries is deliberately absent:
there is no org.freedesktop.portal.NFC yet, so there are no permissions to
show. It slots into the same page when there is.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
